    "q-223601": {
      "dom_id": "step-q223601",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14193754/assets/5835b3fdf2fccfd3b27f326bd90188a8382ea826a019c161e65c8a58376b3d7f.png)\n\nWhat is the range of the function $f(x),$ shown above?\n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\le \\,f(x)$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the lowest and highest $y$-values in the graph.\n\n- The lowest $y$-value is $-2.$ This occurs at the point $(-4,-2).$\n\n- The highest $y$-value is $4.$ This occurs at the point $(4,4).$ However note that this point is not actually included in the graph.\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $-2$ and $4,$ including $-2$ but not including $4.$\n\nTherefore, the range is $-2\\le y<4,$ which can also be written as $-2\\le f(x)<4.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
